      "thinking": "While the investigation pinned the fault on the dependency, the real issue in io-shop's code is that it calls the pricing service with no timeout or bound, letting a slow dependency stall the entire request path\u2014even though pricing isn't essential to rendering the core page content.\n\nThe likely fix is to give the pricing call a timeout budget, so a slow dependency degrades gracefully into a missing basket total (reported like cache_failure) instead of blocking every request. But the current design explicitly waits without retrying or self-pricing, and the seam `ask(shopper_id)` is synchronous, only reporting `took_ms` after the fact\u2014so I need to figure out how to impose a deadline without changing the seam's calling contract too drastically.\n\nA circuit-breaker approach could track slow calls and fail fast on subsequent ones, but that introduces global state which feels ugly for this codebase's shape. The cleanest fix would be to actually thread a timeout through the seam itself, but that changes its signature and breaks existing callers and tests that construct simple one-argument lambdas.\n\nI'm now weighing whether to change the seam's type to accept a timeout parameter alongside the shopper id, which means updating both the account page caller and the test doubles to match this new contract.\n\nI'm considering a simpler alternative: rather than reshaping the call signature, introduce a fixed pricing budget constant and have basket_total degrade gracefully\u2014returning a priced basket with no total and a \"timed out\" marker when the service exceeds that budget, letting the account page render without the price and surface a delay notice instead of raising an error.\n\nThat would spike error rates further, so maybe raising isn't the answer here. I think the real fix is trading a hard failure for bounded latency\u2014keep failing when there's genuinely no price, but make sure the timeout itself resolves quickly rather than hanging the page, since the incident is really about latency, not the existing no-price failure behavior which seems intentional.\n\nActually, treating a timeout as a degraded render feels better: let the page render without basket_total and surface a pricing_delay indicator instead, keeping the page up with bounded latency, similar to how cache_failure is handled elsewhere. I'll define a PRICING_TIMEOUT_MS constant and the pricing service call signature to support this.\n\nWorking out the return shape for basket_total...\n\nGood, account_page needs no change since it already uses `int | None` and doesn't call the pricing seam directly. But I should check other callers\u2014rollout.py, visits.py, typical_spend.py\u2014for any other usages of `ask_the_pricing_service` or `PricingAnswer` before assuming this refactor is isolated.\n\nNow I'm designing the tests that prove the bug: one asserting the shop bounds the wait by passing a deadline (a fake service records the timeout it received, and the current single-arg `ask` would raise TypeError against a two-arg fake), and another confirming a timed-out pricing call doesn't fail the page render.\n\nI also want a latency-bound test \u2014 simulate a service that would take 1500ms but honors the deadline, returning at the timeout with no price, demonstrating bounded latency. Then I need to update the existing pricing and account page tests since the seam changed, and check rollout.py for other pricing callers.\n\n",
